From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mempage4k <mempage4k@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wgpu-core: a pass's barriers first in its own command buffer

Where command buffers cannot be suspended (Vulkan, since wgpu-hal 0005
left it out), each render pass took two raw command buffers again: its
own, and one for the barriers before it, recorded after the pass because
only then are its resources known. On a Pixel 8a (Mali) the second one
cost ~16 ms of CPU a frame (~54 passes): 34 -> 50 ms of encoding, 29 ->
25 fps.

A render pass's resources are known before it is recorded when nothing
but its attachments and the bind groups, vertex and index buffers its
commands set adds a usage: no queries, timestamps, bundles or indirect
draws, and no discarded surface to clear first. Such a pass now merges
those into a usage scope and records its barriers at the start of its own
command buffer, just before the render pass begins (a hook in
RenderPassInfo::start). What it then finds after the pass is nothing new.

And a pass that needs nothing before it (no barriers, clears, query
resets or indirect draw validation) takes no command buffer for it.

Measured on Metal with the tracker doing the same work (its barriers
record nothing there): 93-94 % of a game's render passes take the first
path, and in none of them did the pass find a barrier after it that the
scan had missed. Metal keeps suspending command buffers (0007), where
barriers go at the end of the one before.
---
